#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[Grab-Bag: Words That Get Mixed Up]]
Maily just differences between words that get mixed up a lot. Not sorted at all. Sorry.

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- #strong[Obići vs. Posetiti] — \"Obići\" \(обићи\) typically means to go around or visit multiple places or sites. Example: \"Volim da obilazim grad i vidim različite znamenitosti.\" \(I enjoy going around the city and seeing different landmarks.\) \"Posetiti\" \(посетити\) usually means to visit or stop by a particular location or place. Example: \"Sutra ćemo posetiti muzej.\" \(Tomorrow we will visit the museum.\)
- #strong[Zaista vs. Baš vs. Jako\/Vrlo] — \"Zaista\" emphasizes truth or certainty. Example: \"Zaista volim ovaj grad.\" \(I truly love this city.\) \"Baš\" conveys precision or particularity. Example: \"Baš sam srećan što si došao.\" \(I\'m really glad you came.\) \"Jako\/Vrlo\" intensify the degree of something. Example: \"Jako sam umoran.\" \(I\'m very tired.\)
- #strong[Približiti se vs. Prići\/Prilaziti] — \"Približiti se\" means to come closer in a general sense. Example: \"Približio sam se prozoru da vidim šta se dešava napolju.\" \(I came closer to the window to see what\'s happening outside.\) \"Prići\/Prilaziti\" suggests a deliberate or purposeful approach. Example: \"Prišao sam prodavcu da mu postavim nekoliko pitanja.\" \(I approached the seller to ask him a few questions.\)
- #strong[Prijati vs. Odgovarati] — \"Prijati\" means to be pleasing or agreeable. Example: \"Ovaj topli čaj mi prija u hladnim danima.\" \(This hot tea is pleasant to me on cold days.\) \"Odgovarati\" means to correspond or match. Example: \"Ova veličina cipele mi odgovara.\" \(This shoe size fits me.\)
- #strong[\"Odvojeno\" vs. \"Razdvojen\"] #emph[\(heading as written in the original — the paragraph that follows is actually about \"praviti se\" vs \"pretvarati se\"; kept exactly as extracted#degmark \)] — \"Praviti se\" means \"to pretend\" or \"to act as if.\" It\'s about intentionally presenting oneself in a certain way that doesn\'t reflect reality. For example: \"Pravila se da je srećna, iako nije bila.\" \"Pretvarati se\" also means \"to pretend\" or \"to act as if.\" It involves behaving in a way that doesn\'t reflect one\'s true feelings or intentions, but it can also imply a more deliberate or prolonged effort to deceive. For example: \"Pretvarao se da ne zna o čemu govorimo kako bi izbegao odgovornost.\"
- #strong[Pre nego li?] — In Serbian, \"pre nego što\" is used with indicative verbs to express a certain sequence of events, while \"pre nego li\" is used with subjunctive verbs to indicate a conditional or uncertain sequence of events.
- #strong[When to use kako as \"as\" or \"since\"] — In Serbian, the word \"kako\" can be used as \"as\" or \"since\" in certain contexts. Examples: \"Kako je vreme prolazilo, ona je postajala sve srećnija.\" \(As time passed, she became happier.\) \"Kako je vukao satove, tako su se sjećanja vraćala.\" \(As he wound the clocks, memories came flooding back.\) \"Kako\" is used to indicate a causal or comparative relationship in a sentence, showing a correlation between two events or states. It is often used to express the idea of \"as\" or \"since\" in English.
- #strong[How does \"a da ne\" work] — The Serbian phrase \"a da ne\" is used to express agreement or willingness. It can be translated to English as \"why not?\" or \"sure.\" 1. \"Hoćemo li da idemo u bioskop?\" - \"A da ne, imamo vremena.\" \(Translation: \"Shall we go to the cinema?\" - \"Sure, we have time.\"\) 2. \"Da li želiš još kafe?\" - \"A da ne, hvala.\" \(Translation: \"Would you like more coffee?\" - \"Why not, thank you.\"\) 3. \"Jesi li spreman za izazov?\" - \"A da ne, ne bojim se.\" \(Translation: \"Are you ready for the challenge?\" - \"Sure, I\'m not afraid.\"\)
- #strong[Negoli? Nekolicina?] — \"Negoli\" is used for comparisons, such as \"Ona je pametnija negoli ja\" \(She is smarter than I am\). \"Nekolicina\" denotes a small number, for instance, \"Nekolicina studenata je završila ispit\" \(A few students passed the exam\).
- #strong[Nezavisnost vs. Neovisnost] — \"Nezavisnost\" in Serbian emphasizes freedom or autonomy from external influence, like \"Država je stekla nezavisnost\" \(The country gained independence\). \"Neovisnost\" in Croatian also denotes independence, as in \"Ova organizacija teži neovisnosti\" \(This organization strives for independence\). Both terms convey the idea of being self-reliant or free from external control.
- #strong[Nevešt vs. Nezgodan] — \"Nevešt\" describes someone lacking skill or proficiency, such as \"On je nevešt u vožnji bicikla\" \(He is clumsy at riding a bike\). \"Nezgodan\" conveys awkwardness or inconvenience, for instance, \"To je nezgodna situacija\" \(That is an awkward situation\).
- #strong[Bitno vs. Važno vs. Nužan] — The adjectives \"bitno,\" \"važno,\" and \"nužan\" in Serbian all convey notions of importance, with nuances. \"Bitno\" signifies something essential or crucial, \"važno\" denotes significance, and \"nužan\" implies necessity.
- #strong[Kao što vs. Kao da] — Same differences as just normally using \"što\" and \"da.\"
- #strong[Why do perception verbs go genitive] — This genitive construction with perception verbs helps to convey the idea that the subject is perceiving or experiencing something that is external to them, thus requiring the genitive case to indicate possession or association.
- #strong[Njim vs. Njime] — Like ovome\/tome.
- #strong[Gen. v. Inst.] — Gen tends to focus more on the physical, exact location, while inst. can be more general or metaphorical.
- #strong[Zastupnik vs. Predstavnik] — \"Zastupnik\" focuses more on the delegated authority to act on behalf of someone or something in a formal or government setting, while \"predstavnik\" emphasizes the role of representing or standing for someone or something.
- #strong[When to use \"otkud\"] — In Serbian, \"odakle\" and \"otkud\" are both used to ask about the origin or starting point of something, but they are used in slightly different contexts. 1. \"Odakle\" \(откуд\) is used as an interrogative pronoun to ask about the source or origin of something. It is more commonly used in standard Serbian and formal speech. Example: \"Odakle si ti?\" \(Откуд си ти?\) - Where are you from? 2. \"Otkud\" \(одкуд\) is also used to inquire about the origin or source of something, but it is more informal and colloquial compared to \"odakle.\" It is often used in spoken Serbian and can convey a sense of informality. Example: \"Otkud to znaš?\" \(Одкуд то знаш?\) - How do you know that? In summary, \"odakle\" is more formal and standard, while \"otkud\" is more informal and colloquial when asking about origins or sources.
- #strong[Naslanjati se vs. Oslanjati se] — In summary, \"oslanjati se\" refers to relying or depending on someone or something, while \"naslanjati se\" means physically leaning or resting on something for support or comfort.
- #strong[Oblast vs. Područje] — Oblast - bigger and administrative. Područje - smaller area, broader term.
- #strong[Poznavati vs. Znati; Paziti vs. Opaziti] — \"Poznavati\" implies familiarity or acquaintance, while \"znati\" refers to possessing knowledge or awareness. Similarly, \"paziti\" involves being careful or attentive, while \"opaziti\" means noticing or observing something.
- #strong[Pružiti vs. Nuditi] — Pružiti refers to offering something to someone physically or offering help\/assistance; Nuditi is offering options or ideas for someone to choose.
- #strong[Obavljati vs. Izvršiti] — \"Obavljati\" emphasizes the ongoing or repeated performance of tasks, \"izvršiti\" focuses on the completion or execution of specific actions.
- #strong[Mnogo + Gen? When and why] — Just does.
- #strong[Od + gen?? When to use] — \"Od + genitive\": This construction is used to indicate the material something is made of or the origin\/source of something.
- #strong[Plod vs. Voće] — In Serbian, \"plod\" and \"voće\" both relate to fruits, but they have different meanings: \"Plod\" refers to a single piece of fruit or the fruit of a plant. It can be used to describe any individual fruit. \"Voće\" is the collective term for fruits in general. It refers to all types of fruits collectively as a category.
]
#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[Summary]]
Here is a summary of the differences between the listed words with examples:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
+ \"Bilo šta\/šta god\": \"Bilo šta\" means \"anything\" and is used in questions, while \"šta god\" means \"whatever.\" Example: \"Da li imaš bilo šta za piće?\" \(Do you have anything to drink?\) Example: \"Šta god da uradiš, biće u redu.\" \(Whatever you do, it will be okay.\)
+ \"Čujem mu glas vs Čujem njegov glas\": \"Čujem mu glas\" implies \"I hear his voice generically,\" while \"Čujem njegov glas\" specifies whose voice is being heard. Example: \"Čujem mu glas preko telefona.\" \(I hear his voice over the phone.\) Example: \"Čujem njegov glas u masi ljudi.\" \(I hear his voice in the crowd of people.\)
]
#strong[Thus answer is cap. D\/L pronouns can be used to show possession in informal situations and between people who use \"ti\"]

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
+ \"Šaliti\/zezati\": \"Šaliti\" means \"to joke\" in a friendly or light-hearted manner, while \"zezati\" can imply teasing or playfully making fun of someone. Example: \"Voli da se šali sa svojim kolegama.\" \(He likes to joke with his colleagues.\) Example: \"Nemoj više da me zezate, nisam raspoložen.\" \(Don\'t tease me anymore, I\'m not in the mood.\)
+ \"Moćan\/snažan\": \"Moćan\" typically refers to power, influence, or capability, while \"snažan\" often refers to physical strength or intensity. Example: \"Moćan lider donosi važne odluke.\" \(A powerful leader makes important decisions.\) Example: \"Sportista mora biti snažan i izdržljiv.\" \(An athlete must be strong and resilient
]
