#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 13pt, weight: "bold")[More Questions]]
#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[When do I use \"da + present tense\" instead of an infinitive?]]
Ok so this trips up literally everyone at some point. Standard Serbian \(and this is even more true the further south\/east you go\) really prefers \"da + present tense\" over the infinitive, especially in speech.#degmark  \"Hoću da idem\" \(\"I want to go\"\) is going to sound way more natural coming out of your mouth than \"Hoću ići\" — the infinitive isn\'t wrong exactly, it\'s just more of a Croatian\/western-Bosnian thing and even there it gets replaced by da-constructions constantly in casual speech.#degmark  If you\'re ever unsure, just default to da + present. You genuinely cannot go wrong leaning on it too much, but you can sound stiff leaning on the infinitive too much.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[How do I know when to use \"koji\" versus just restructuring the sentence?]]
\"Koji\" is your relative pronoun \(\"who\/which\/that\"\) and it has to agree in gender\/number with whatever it\'s replacing, then case-decline based on its job in its own clause#degmark  — that\'s the part that fries people\'s brains because those two things \(agreement and case\) come from two totally different places in the sentence. Ngl the easiest fix when you blank on the correct form mid-sentence is to just restart the clause with \"koji\" in the nominative and rebuild around it, or straight up avoid it and use two shorter sentences instead. Natives do this too when a \"koji\" clause is getting long and clunky — nobody\'s grading you on relative clause elegance.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[Is there a shortcut for guessing a verb\'s other aspect pair?]]
Kind of, not a hard rule tho. A huge chunk of imperfective→perfective pairs just add a prefix \(pisati → napisati, raditi → uraditi\) and you saw a wall of that a few pages back.#degmark  The annoying ones are where the perfective changes the verb stem entirely instead of prefixing it — govoriti\/reći is the classic example, and there\'s no way to guess that one, you just gotta learn it as its own pair.#degmark  My rule of thumb: if a prefixed version \"feels like\" it should exist and means basically the same thing but \"done,\" it probably does exist. If it feels forced, it\'s probably a suppletive pair \(different root\) and you just have to memorize it.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[What\'s the deal with clitics jumping to second position in a sentence?]]
This one\'s called \"second-position clitics\" and it\'s one of those rules that natives do 100% automatically and will never be able to explain to you.#degmark  Basically your short unstressed words — je, sam, ću, se, mi, ga, and friends — want to sit in the second slot of the clause, right after the first stressed word or phrase, no matter what part of the sentence that first word belongs to.#degmark  So \"Video sam ga juče\" is normal, but you\'ll also hear stuff like \"Juče sam ga video\" — the clitic cluster just glues itself to whatever\'s up front. When you\'ve got multiple clitics stacking \(reflexive se, auxiliary sam, dative pronoun mi\) there\'s also a pecking order to which comes first, and honestly the fastest way to get that order into your head is just repetition, not memorizing the rule.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[Should I default to \"u\" or \"na\" and how do I know which case goes with it?]]
Both \"u\" and \"na\" are two-case prepositions — accusative when there\'s motion into\/onto something, locative when you\'re just sitting there existing in\/on it.#degmark  \"Idem u prodavnicu\" \(I\'m going into the store, accusative\) vs \"Ja sam u prodavnici\" \(I\'m in the store, locative\). Some nouns just conventionally pair with \"na\" instead of \"u\" for no reason you can logic your way to — \"na fakultetu,\" \"na poslu,\" \"na stanici\" — and that\'s just something you absorb from hearing it, not something you can derive. When in doubt, ask yourself \"is anything moving here?\" first, and worry about u-vs-na second.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[Do I default to Ijekavian or Ekavian, and what happens if I mix them?]]
This is genuinely regional and honestly a little political, so tread respectfully. Ekavian \(mleko, reč, sneg\) is standard in Serbia; Ijekavian \(mlijeko, riječ, snijeg\) is standard in Croatia, Bosnia, and Montenegro.#degmark  Nobody\'s going to be shocked that a foreigner mixes them sometimes, but mixing them mid-sentence out of laziness \(not out of quoting someone\) reads as sloppy rather than charming, so try to pick one per conversation based on where you actually are and stick with it.#degmark  The words that don\'t alternate at all \(remember zapovijed\/zapovest from earlier\) are the ones that\'ll actually embarrass you if you get them wrong, so those are worth memorizing as fixed forms rather than trying to \"convert\" them on the fly.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[Do I use \"vi\" or \"ti\" with someone I just met, like an investigator?]]
Default to \"vi\" with anyone you don\'t already have an established informal relationship with — that includes basically every new investigator, every less-active you\'re meeting for the first time, older members, anyone in a position of authority.#degmark  It is way less awkward to get invited to switch to \"ti\" \(see the phrases up above\) than to presumptuously use \"ti\" on someone who was expecting \"vi\" — that second one can genuinely read as disrespectful. Kids are the obvious exception, always \"ti\" with kids. When people your own age offer the switch, take it, it\'s usually a genuine gesture of warmth, not just politeness.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[What do I do when I flat out don\'t know the word for something?]]
Circumlocution is your best friend and honestly one of the most useful skills you can build, more useful than half the vocab lists tbh. Describe the object\/idea using words you do know — what it\'s for, what it looks like, what category it\'s in, gesture if you have to.#degmark  \"Ono što koristiš da...\" \(that thing you use to...\) will get you unbelievably far. Natives are almost always willing to fill in the word once they get what you\'re going for, and you\'ll retain that word way better because you had to work for it than if you\'d just looked it up. Don\'t let not knowing one word derail the whole sentence — talk around it and keep moving.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[How do I guess the gender of a noun I\'ve never seen before?]]
Not a perfect rule but a genuinely useful heuristic: nouns ending in a consonant are usually masculine, nouns ending in \"-a\" are usually feminine, and nouns ending in \"-o\" or \"-e\" are usually neuter.#degmark  The exceptions are the ones that bite you — stuff like \"gazda\" and \"tata\" from earlier in this chapter that end in \"-a\" but are masculine because of what they refer to, and feminine nouns ending in a soft consonant like \"-ost\" \(radost, mladost\) that don\'t end in \"-a\" at all but are still feminine.#degmark  If you\'re not sure, just watch what adjective ending gets used with it in conversation — that\'ll tell you the gender faster than trying to reason it out from the noun ending alone.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[What\'s the difference between \"da li\" and \"je li\" for yes\/no questions?]]
Both turn a statement into a yes\/no question, they\'re regionally flavored more than meaningfully different. \"Da li\" is the safe, standard, works-everywhere option.#degmark  \"Je li\" \(sometimes just clipped to \"je l\'\" in fast speech\) leans more colloquial and you\'ll hear it a ton in casual conversation, especially in Bosnia and parts of Serbia.#degmark  You can also just flip the verb and subject with rising intonation and skip both of them entirely \(\"Ideš u grad?\"\) which honestly is what people default to most in relaxed conversation anyway. Don\'t overthink which one to use — \"da li\" will never sound wrong.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[Am I overusing diminutives without realizing it?]]
Probably, ngl, everyone does at first because they\'re fun and they make you feel like you\'re bonding with people. Diminutives \(kafa → kafica, pas → psić\) genuinely do soften a sentence and signal warmth, but stacking them onto every noun in a sentence starts to sound like you\'re talking to a toddler rather than an adult.#degmark  Save them for the moments they\'re actually doing something — offering someone coffee, talking about a kid or a pet, being affectionate — rather than as a default setting on every noun. If you notice a native\'s eyebrow twitch when you diminutive-ify something mundane like a form or a bus schedule, that\'s your sign you went too far.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[Do numbers change the case of the noun after them?]]
Yeah and this one\'s genuinely annoying at first. \"Jedan\" \(one\) works like a normal adjective and just agrees with its noun. \"Dva\/tri\/četiri\" \(two\/three\/four\) take the noun in a special paucal form that looks like genitive singular for masculine\/neuter nouns.#degmark  \"Pet\" and up take genitive plural.#degmark  So you get \"jedan sto\" \(one table\), \"dva stola\" \(two tables — that\'s genitive singular, not nominative plural\), \"pet stolova\" \(five tables — genitive plural\). It feels like three different systems duct-taped together because it kind of is, but once you drill a few nouns through all three buckets it clicks surprisingly fast.

